From cdc4704d51a79b7022db2e0a414c821163431c2d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CipherScope <dev@cipherscope.io>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 11:19:15 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] signet: let a custom signet retarget against a shorter target
 spacing from a height on

A signet keeps Bitcoin's difficulty adjustment: every 2016 blocks the
target is multiplied by the time the last period took over the time it
was expected to take (2016 * 600 s), with that ratio clamped to between
a quarter and four. A custom signet whose producer runs faster than
every ten minutes has its difficulty raised at each retarget, by the
full factor while a period takes under a quarter of two weeks, until the
proof of work on top of the producer's pause brings the interval back to
ten minutes. Ark-0 (a block every 90 s plus the grind) went 1e0377ae ->
1e00ddeb at height 4032 -> 1d377ac0 at 6048, and its average spacing
from 94.9 s to 168.4 s.

-signetpowtargetspacing=<seconds>[@<height>] makes the retargets at or
above <height> (default 0) measure each period against <seconds> per
block (1 to 600) instead. The retarget interval stays 2016 blocks, so
the change is a different expected timespan and nothing else:
Consensus::Params gains pow_target_spacing_change and
PowTargetTimespan(height), and CalculateNextWorkRequired and
PermittedDifficultyTransition read the timespan through it.
nPowTargetSpacing stays 600 for everything else that reads it. Every
other chain, and a signet without the option, keeps nPowTargetTimespan
everywhere. The option requires -signetchallenge, and the node logs the
values at startup. The upper bound is 600 because signet's powLimit
leaves room for the retarget's 256-bit product (old target times up to
four times the timespan) at 2016 blocks of 600 s and not at 601.

A header is checked when it is first accepted, not when the block index
is loaded, so a node given the option after it accepted a header at or
above a retarget the option changes, or one whose option was removed or
changed later, would keep headers its current rule rejects and could
follow a chain that a fresh node with the same rule rejects.
RetargetConflicts() in pow.cpp asks whether a header is a retarget whose
nBits differs from the one the node's current rule gives it, and on
every signet CompleteChainstateInitialization asks it of every valid
header, not only the active chain's, right after the block index is
loaded: a conflict refuses startup, as the witness redownload check
does, naming the lowest conflicting height and asking for the setting it
was accepted under to be restored or for -reindex. On a signet whose
rule never changed it finds nothing. doc/signet-target-spacing.md
describes the option and what a running network has to do to adopt it.

Based on the consensus series alone, so it applies on top of it with or
without the M0.5 wallet series.

Tests: pow_tests checks that no chain has the change by default; that
powLimit leaves room for the product at 600 s and not at 601; on a
custom signet with 90@8064, Ark-0's own retarget at 6048 (234,329 s over
4032..6047: clamped, target / 4 -> 1d377ac0), the same period at the
change height (target * 234,329 / 181,440 -> 1e011e9b), both clamps at
the new timespan (1d377ac0 accepted and 1d377abf refused; 1e0377ac
accepted and 1e0377ad, still below powLimit, refused), saturation at
powLimit, that 90@8065 changes the retarget at 10080 and not the one at
8064, and that the target does not move between retargets. A second case
builds 4033 blocks through GetNextWorkRequired under the old rule and
under 90@4032 (1e00ddeb and 1e03773d at 4032) and checks that
RetargetConflicts finds exactly the retarget at 4032 under 90@4032 and
under 90@0 (whose retarget at 2016 coincides with the old rule's),
nothing under the rule a chain was built with or under 90@4033, and the
retarget at 4032 again when the 90@4032 chain is checked with the option
removed or moved to 4033. feature_p2mr_signet covers the missing
challenge, malformed values (empty, signs, whitespace, a second '@',
overflow of either half), values out of range, a negative height, a bare
option (read as empty), repetition, negation, and a start and restart
with the option. The startup refusal itself is not covered by a
functional test: reaching a retarget takes 2016 signet blocks at
signet's minimum difficulty, which is too slow for the suite.
